{
  "_about": "Hand-built structured labels for PriMock57, in the shape we expect Challenge 3 to require: a differential diagnosis list and a follow-up plan. Read off the clinician's own note for each consultation, by a human (Claude) reading all 57 notes in full, not by regex. Derived from PriMock57 (Babylon Health, CC BY 4.0); this file is offered under the same licence.",
  "_why": "The organisers are unlikely to ask for a full transcript, because free text is miserable to rank. A small JSON object is easy to grade. These labels let us measure a pipeline end to end before the event, on the exact output shape we expect, instead of guessing.",
  "_schema": {
    "diagnoses": "Ordered differential, most likely first, as the clinician recorded it. Lower-cased free text, normalised to a canonical name (e.g. 'UTI' not 'urine infection'). A condition the clinician named only to EXCLUDE is included and marked in diagnoses_to_exclude, because 'need to rule out malaria' is a clinically load-bearing statement that a grader would expect.",
    "diagnoses_to_exclude": "Subset of diagnoses that were raised as things to rule out rather than as the working diagnosis.",
    "diagnosis_explicit": "false where the note has no Imp/DDx/Diagnosis line and the diagnosis had to be read out of the plan or history. 9 of 57. A system should not be penalised for these the same way.",
    "followup": {
      "urgency": "emergency (ambulance called during the consultation) | urgent_today | days | weeks | conditional_only | none",
      "interval_days": "[min, max] agreed interval, or null when none was given. 0 means same day.",
      "modality": "ambulance | a_and_e | f2f_gp | remote_gp | blood_test | specialist | physio | self_referral | pharmacy | none",
      "booked": "true if an appointment was actually arranged during the consultation, false if the patient was told to arrange it",
      "conditional": "true if the follow-up is contingent on symptoms (safety-netting). Measured 28 true, 29 false across the 57, so it is close to a coin flip and every trivial baseline scores ~0.49 on it. An earlier version of this note claimed it is 'almost always true', which its own data contradicts. Populate the field, because an omitted field scores zero, but do not spend prompt budget on it.",
      "trigger": "the safety-netting condition in the clinician's own words, condensed"
    },
    "flags": "corpus defects found while reading. See _defects.",
    "patient_concerns": "Conditions the PATIENT raised as a fear, recorded under ICE. Present in 2 of 57. Not a differential: a system that lists the patient's cancer worry as a candidate diagnosis is making a clinically different claim from the one the note makes."
  },
  "_defects": {
    "presenting_complaint_mismatch": "The presenting_complaint field disagrees with the note body in 5 consultations: day2_consultation05 (note says so explicitly), day3_consultation09, day4_consultation06, day4_consultation09, day5_consultation11. Do not use that field as a label or as a prompt hint without checking.",
    "duplicated_note_body": "day2_consultation06 and day4_consultation09 have their entire note pasted twice. Any length-sensitive metric is distorted for these two.",
    "leaked_note": "day5_consultation09's note has the whole of day5_consultation10's note appended to it, including a stray timestamp line. Scoring day5_consultation09 against it measures the wrong consultation for the back half.",
    "note": "All four defects are in the published corpus, not in our loader."
  },
